Chapter 10-1　　『Friends』
“—That really does sound like that princess, huh. Going all the way to the end doing whatever the hell she wanted.”
Felt said so while bringing the teacup to her lips. Emilia's eyes softened at the corners in response.
Across from her, Felt sat on the reception sofa with one knee drawn up. She had listened to Emilia's story all the way through without interruption and mourned Priscilla in her own characteristic way.
Emilia didn't think Felt heartless for the fact that no great sorrow or sense of loss passed through those crimson eyes—slightly different in hue from Priscilla's. Instead, she simply thought: She's strong.
Including the fact that Felt had come here on her own initiative to hear the details of Priscilla's death.
Right now, the room where Emilia and Felt faced each other was one of the chambers in the royal residence in the royal capital of Lugunica. It was the reception room of the mansion assigned for important figures staying in the capital, and Emilia had welcomed Felt here.
They hadn't arranged to meet. Felt, who happened to be in the capital, had heard that Emilia had brought news to the royal castle and came to see her like this.
Honestly, Emilia was very happy that Felt had come to visit.
To begin with, the Emilia Camp owed Felt a huge debt—according to Anastasia, during the time they were away from the kingdom, Meili had been greatly taken care of by Felt's side.
Annerose, a relative of Roswaal and a supporter of Emilia, had been entrusted with various negotiations regarding Meili and the Pleiades Watchtower. Apparently, Felt and her group had lent significant help in making those negotiations succeed.
Because of that, Emilia had planned to make time to go thank Felt personally, so being able to meet like this was a huge help.
Still—if only she could have brought better news…
“—You look like you're thinking something like that, Emilia-neechan.”
“Huh? Was my face that easy to read?”
“Pretty damn obvious. You get that wrinkle between your brows and look down like that, it's instant.”
Felt tapped her own brow with a finger; Emilia unconsciously rubbed hers the same way.
People often said her thoughts showed easily on her face, but she was surprised at herself for it being this obvious. And anxious too. —She had decided she was completely recovered now, no problem at all. She wouldn't worry everyone anymore; she had steeled herself like that.
“But this won't do. Subaru and the others will get mad at me.”
“That bro getting mad and raising his horns at you, Emilia-neechan? Can't even picture it.”
“No way. Subaru needs me to be reliable, or he'd be in trouble. So if I have bad points, he tells me straight.”
“Hmm, wonder how real that is.”
Felt laughed, resting her chin on her raised knee and flashing her double fangs.
Emilia pursed her lips at Felt's somewhat teasing, mean-spirited smile, then continued with “But—”
“It seems like my thoughts were totally visible to Felt-chan… but you don't feel that way yourself?”
“Little complicated, but you mean… even though it's been a while since we last met face-to-face, the topic's the princess's death and you're sorry about that?”
“Um, yeah… that's right. Yes, exactly.”
“On that note, I'd actually say I'm glad I heard it from you, Emilia-neechan.”
“From me?”
Surprised by the unexpected words, Emilia asked back. Felt nodded “Yeah” while scratching her head in front of the wide-eyed Emilia.
“Honestly, when I first heard that princess died, I thought it was a joke. She was like the poster child for 'you can't kill her even if you try.' If it stayed that way, I'd just keep making up random crap in my head, and the real facts would've gotten buried.”
“—”
“So hearing the actual story from you, who was right there, Emilia-neechan… it let me put it behind me. …Well, more like it prepared me to put it behind me. Tch, now I'm talking complicated too. Sorry.”
“—. No, I think I get what Felt-chan means.”
Felt made a sour face, unable to explain it well, but Emilia felt the sentiment.
If the roles were reversed—if Emilia heard far away that someone she knew had died without her knowing the details—she'd probably fill her head with all sorts of imaginings about the death.
Some right, some wrong, some impossible to confirm, until the real truth got lost.
So even if it was painful, having that space cut off for her was a relief.
“And if it's from someone you trust, it's even better.”
“…That makes it sound like I'm the one who trusts you, Emilia-neechan?”
“Yes. I trust Felt-chan too… and you feel the same, right? That's why you came to see me, isn't it?”
“We're rival candidates, y'know! Nodding here would be weird!”
Felt lowered her legs from the chair, propped her cheek on her hand, and gave an exasperated look. But since she didn't shake her head, Emilia smiled in relief.
Thinking of Priscilla still made Emilia's chest ache dully. But being able to keep Felt's hazy feelings from staying buried—that felt good.
“—”
A faint breath cut into their exchange. Felt, sharp-eared, immediately caught it and said “Oi” in an annoyed tone.
“What're you grinning at?”
“—No, forgive me. It was just… seeing Felt-sama being so thoroughly outmatched by Emilia-sama was so rare, I couldn't help it.”
“'Couldn't help it' my ass. What kinda knight smirks while watching his master get done in?”
“Please forgive me. I hesitated to interrupt the two of you.”
The one who said so, placing a hand on his chest and bowing, was Felt's knight—Reinhard, with his distinctive red hair and blue eyes.
Naturally, Felt wouldn't come visit Emilia alone; he had been listening to the whole conversation. At the friendly exchange between Reinhard and Felt, Emilia prefaced with “Jeez” and
“You don't have to be that considerate. Our Otto-kun has been here listening the whole time too… wait, he wasn't?”
“Indeed. Thank you for noticing. Even I have enough sense not to butt into conversation between two people of such high standing, you know.”
The one who smiled wryly beside the surprised, brow-raised Emilia was Otto, who had been there all along.
This time, only Otto had accompanied Emilia to the capital, so naturally he was with her for the report to the castle and meetings at the residence. His mere presence was reassuring, so she'd completely relaxed even when he wasn't speaking.
“Sorry, I didn't notice. Otto-kun loves talking, so staying quiet must've been super hard.”
“If I may, I only speak when necessary in the necessary moments—I'm not actually a chatterbox, you know!”
“Oho, that's some weak convincing. A guy who chats not just with humans but bugs and water dragons and whatever, saying he ain't talkative? Come on.”
“Please don't mix up my Divine Protection with my personality.”
Otto pressed down on his green hat and replied to the casual Felt.
With the power of his Divine Protection of Soul Language letting him talk to all sorts of creatures, Felt's jab hit the mark—Emilia felt. Come to think of it, wherever he went, Otto was always talking to animals, bugs, every living thing, expanding his circle of friends.
“Otto-kun has sooo many friends. I don't have that many, so I really respect how you can get along with anyone.”
“'Friends' is a bit off, actually. Most of the time what I'm offering is negotiation or deals—different skillset from making friends. So thinking that way, I'm probably not the 'lots of friends' type either.”
“Thinking hard about what counts as a buddy is pointless crap. If anything, go be friends with our Reinhard. He's got few friends too.”
“Eh, really? That's super surprising.”
Felt winked one eye and pointed at Reinhard standing behind the sofa; Emilia couldn't help looking up at the red-haired knight.
Reinhard was friendly, super famous—surely everyone in the kingdom knew his name and relied on him. Ever since they first met, he'd been nothing but kind to Emilia, so she'd thought he was friends with the entire kingdom.
To Emilia's surprised gaze, Reinhard continued with “Unfortunately,”
“Even if I approach them, I seem to make people shrink back a lot. Excluding knights, camp members… Subaru, Ferris… my friends may be few.”
“Really. Even Reinhard has worries like that… Hey, Otto-kun, if it's okay—would you be friends with Reinhard?”
“This is an outrageously huge request! Wouldn't this be dishonorable from Reinhard-san's perspective!?”
“No way. I'd welcome it, actually. Will you be my friend?”
“This feels more like a pincer attack—or a sneak attack?”
Sandwiched between Emilia's gaze and Reinhard's smile, Otto went “Guh” in distress.
Felt alone watched with a grin, but after long hesitation, Otto let out a deep breath.
“As Felt-sama said earlier, we're from rival camps right now. Until the Royal Selection ends, we should avoid getting overly close.”
“I see. Regrettable, but if that's your decision, I'll respect it. Not as a friend, though.”
“Yes, please do. Since we're not friends.”
Otto drooped his shoulders saying something lonely; Reinhard responded with a smile that somehow didn't look hurt or disappointed. Unfortunately they couldn't become friends—but to Emilia, they somehow had the atmosphere of friends anyway.
Plus—
“Goodness, the tea's gone completely cold. I'll brew a fresh pot. Emilia-sama, Felt-sama, please continue enjoying your chat. I—”
“—. Then maybe I'll join you. We may not be friends, but allow me to show you the effect of my Divine Protection of Tea Ceremony.”
“You're surprisingly grudge-holding… and having the 'Sword Saint' hold a grudge doesn't feel survivable.”
Chatting like that, the two left the room.
Watching their backs disappear behind the closing door, Emilia turned back to Felt.
“They seemed super much like friends, didn't they?”
“Kukuh, yeah, I thought so too. Usually that Reinhard guy's attitude pisses me off, but seeing it as an outsider is hilarious.”
“Ah, evil face. But because of our Royal Selection, making Otto-kun hold back from being Reinhard's friend…”
“Hah, that bro just conveniently used my words as an excuse. No clue why… but 'can't get close 'cause of the Selection'—don't take that crap seriously.”
“Don't take it seriously… good.”
Felt seemed in an exceptionally good mood—almost humming—from how entertaining it was. After thinking a bit, Emilia said “Okay” and stood up.
Otto, thinking of the Selection, had given up on becoming Reinhard's friend. She felt bad for his decision and consideration, but Emilia had decided.
Because of Priscilla—she didn't want to have any more regrets.
“Hey, Felt-chan, is it okay?”
“Oho? What's up?”
Emilia stepped right up and sat down beside Felt, who looked up at her. At close enough distance for their shoulders to touch, she faced Felt head-on. Felt blinked her red eyes at the sudden approach.
Come to think of it, the first time Emilia met Felt was when Felt stole the Royal Selection badge she needed to qualify. Her mind had gone blank, and even though the situation was like that, Pack didn't panic at all—she'd almost taken it out on him. Anyway, her meeting with Felt definitely didn't start well.
Even so, now Emilia thought this toward Felt, a fellow Royal Selection candidate.
“I want to become friends with Felt-chan. Not just regular friends—super close best friends.”
“…Hah?”
“Actually, Anastasia-san and I promised to become friends after the Selection ends. Traveling together, talking about all sorts of things… well, it wasn't really Anastasia-san the whole time, but we talked a lot and made that promise.”
Felt's big eyes wavered in confusion; Emilia pressed on briskly.
The promise with Anastasia—since she strongly identified as a merchant, she'd definitely keep it. Unlike Subaru, she could trust a promise with her.
“Subaru breaks promises right away so I'm worried, but Anastasia-san is reliable. But even unrelated to promises, I don't feel like I can be friends with Subaru, and I don't think I want to be friends with him either… huh? What was it again?”
“Emilia-neechan saying she can't be friends with Subaru-bro?”
“Yes, that's it… No, not that—our talk, Felt-chan and me!”
Emilia got chills at almost losing the main point. Right before that happened, Felt suddenly looked up. Following the motion, Emilia looked at the ceiling too. Nothing in particular.
“Felt-chan?”
“Not looking at the ceiling. Just thinking what to dooo.”
“Um, right. Subaru and Petra-chan always tell me that looking at my face cheers them up a lot. How about it?”
“Are you seriously trying to sell me your good points right now?”
“Yes, because I want to be friends with Felt-chan.”
Over this past year-plus, Emilia had gotten used to looking at her own face in the mirror.
Subaru, Petra, and sometimes Ram praised her face—so maybe it would work on Felt too; she tried it as a long shot.
To that desperate approach from Emilia, Felt—still looking up—said
“Why do you want to be friends with me that bad? We're fine as is…”
“Because I wanted to be friends with Priscilla.”
“—”
“But I kept putting off telling her. I'm hopeless. Even though I knew a precious person who was fine yesterday might suddenly be gone forever.”
Emilia clutched her chest tightly and touched the magic crystal hanging from her neck as she murmured.
The same went for Pack, who continued sleeping. The same for her precious family frozen in ice in the Elior Forest. Separation came suddenly—even to the most important people.
Priscilla's parting reminded the forgetful Emilia of that sad fact.
“I don't want to forget it anymore. So I've decided not to hold back. Telling the people I like that I like them. Telling the people I want to be friends with that I want to be friends.”
“—Damn, that princess went and did it right to the very end.”
“—?”
Emilia had spoken her feelings as honestly as possible. Felt—who had listened while still looking up—slowly lowered her gaze.
The expression on Felt's face wasn't regretful or bitter—it was a smile.
“Got it. Fine. I'll be friends with you too, Emilia-neechan.”
“Really!? Does that mean after the Selection—”
“No need to wait. If it's gonna happen anyway, I'll become friends with you before Anastasia-neechan and make her jealous.”
“Ah, don't say that. Anastasia-san is going to be my friend too.”
“But since I'm already your friend and Anastasia-neechan isn't yet—doesn't that put me higher in your heart right now?”
“Jeez, stop teasing!”
Felt cackled, flashing her double fangs; Emilia couldn't help smiling too.
Felt had given her a happy reply to her proposal. The realization that she had become friends with Felt slowly filled Emilia's chest with warm feelings.
While laughing together with Felt like that, Emilia thought—of Al, who still couldn't fully accept Priscilla's death even now, and Subaru and the others staying by his side.
“With Al too—”
Someday. Not right now, but someday.
She earnestly hoped for the day when she could talk about Priscilla—who she had wished to become friends with—without tears, with some other expression.
Yes—Emilia thought so while leaning shoulder-to-shoulder with her friend.
　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　　△▼△▼△▼△
“—And you even gave me a look. You had something to talk about, right?”
After leaving Emilia and Felt alone in the reception room and walking down the corridor a ways, Reinhard suddenly broached the subject.
Carrying the silver tray with the tea set, Otto nodded without turning. “Yes.”
“I'm glad you noticed. With Felt-sama visiting, Emilia-sama finally managed a natural smile. I didn't want to break that atmosphere.”
“As I thought, Priscilla-sama's matter hit her quite hard.”
“Honestly, I doubt anyone who participated in that battle wasn't hit hard. Even I—with almost no connection—feel pretty crushed.”
Of course, the shock Otto felt couldn't compare to Emilia—who had favorable feelings toward Priscilla—or Subaru and Al, who watched her vanish right before them.
Still—for Otto, who had acted intending to bring back as many allies as possible from the battle alongside the 『Great Disaster』—the death of a kingdom-related figure, a Royal Selection candidate, hit hard. At the same time, a part of him coldly analyzed the elimination of a rival candidate with abnormal charisma.
“…Surprising.”
As if reading Otto's inner thoughts, Reinhard murmured so. Glancing back, Otto saw Reinhard—with eyebrows raised in genuine surprise, just as he'd said.
At that reaction, Otto gave a slightly self-deprecating smile at the corner of his mouth.
“That surprise—is because I felt pain at Priscilla-sama's death? Certainly, saying I have zero desire to welcome a rival's elimination would be a lie…”
“No. What surprised me was you honestly voicing your own vulnerability. You don't have to deliberately make yourself out to be so villainous.”
“—”
“Or… is confessing your true feelings proof you've let your guard down… a sign we could become friends?”
“Seems I chose my words poorly.”
Teased, Otto squared his shoulders a bit and resumed walking.
Apparently his heart was in worse shape than he realized. Realizing that, Reinhard had let the slip-up slide as mere banter.
“Pull yourself together, Otto Suwen.”
He couldn't stay like this—he tightened his own reins.
No matter how little hostility Felt had toward Emilia, they were still rivals. Better not to show weakness. Letting someone see his mental balance crumbling was among the absolute worst things he could do.
Conscious of that again, Otto fulfilled his purpose in drawing Reinhard out.
That was—
“—I'll share information regarding Heinkel Astrea.”
“—”
The corridor's air clearly changed at the broached topic.
Dry or damp—humidity shifted. Hot or cold—temperature changed. And the only certainty was that the one causing it was the 『Sword Saint』 walking right beside him.
“Heinkel-san participated in the war in the Empire as a member of Priscilla-sama's camp and survived. However, after being seen greatly distraught following Priscilla-sama's death, his whereabouts became unknown.”
“…Captured by the Empire?”
“No, unlikely. While they can be utterly ruthless for profit, right now—exhausted from that war—they're not foolish enough to make an enemy of the kingdom… of 『Sword Saint』 Reinhard van Astrea… without clear benefit.”
“Then he vanished of his own accord.”
Reinhard faintly lowered his eyes and murmured powerlessly; Otto listened.
He hadn't meant it as payback, but the result was the same as his own earlier slip—he'd created a situation disrupting the other's mental balance. Still, he felt no urge to exploit or probe that weakness now.
Honestly, he thought himself unsuited for this—but—
“Heinkel-san… your father… are you worried?”
The question came from genuine concern for someone who had shown him consideration.
It was surely a hard question for Reinhard to answer. Otto didn't know the details of their relationship, but it was common knowledge that the father-son bond was fractured and broken.
Moreover, Otto had witnessed—in Priestella—the scene where Heinkel held a sword to Felt to restrain Reinhard's movements.
If going that far, the fact that all three generations supported different Royal Selection candidates outright showed the Astrea house's dysfunction.
Regardless—
“Whether it's comforting or not, according to Garfiel, your father endured even a blow from the 『Dragon』. So the chance he's in life-threatening danger from an attack seems low.”
While conveying that, Otto deliberately avoided mentioning the possibility of Heinkel's suicide.
From what he'd heard, Heinkel—distraught over Priscilla's death—had even tried to shift his loyalty to Emilia and begged to serve her. Refused by Emilia and with nowhere to go, the chance he might do something drastic in despair wasn't zero—
“—That won't happen, Otto. My father would never take his own life.”
“…Did it show on my face like with Emilia-sama?”
Otto rubbed his brow with one hand as his inner thoughts were pinpointed. Reinhard gave a wry smile and said “No,”
“I thought you deliberately avoided the worst possibility out of consideration. But that worry is needless. My father has a goal he absolutely must live to accomplish. Choosing death without fulfilling it—that's impossible for him.”
“If so, that's reassuring… but also worrying. If he's cornered, who knows what he'll do… Maybe next he'll approach Felt-sama?”
For Heinkel, that would be far more viable than clinging to Emilia.
Of course, doing so would negate his one absolute advantage—the ability to exert some coercion against 『Sword Saint』 Reinhard. But even without that, it was a highly likely option for being taken in.
Having lost Priscilla and been rejected by Emilia, Otto thought it entirely possible Heinkel would choose Felt as his next host.
However—
“—That won't happen either.”
The tone was even more certain than before, silencing Otto's next words.
“—”
A shadow like night mixed into those clear blue eyes reflecting the sky. The trace of a smile vanished from his mouth; Reinhard walked silently beside the quiet Otto.
Two steps, three—they closed the distance to the kitchen without words. The conversation seemed about to end in silence, but
“My father wants me to quit being 『Sword Saint』. So in this Royal Selection he absolutely must win, he can't possibly lend strength to Felt-sama, whom I support.”
“…He wants to win the Selection. But you don't want him to win?”
“Exactly. On that one point, Father and I are incompatible. No matter how he wishes it, I am the 『Sword Saint』. —I must be the 『Sword Saint』.”
Everyone had their circumstances.
Otto understood that much, so he had no intention of meddling in another's family affairs. Even if it felt undeniably twisted as parent and child—and even if that last line sounded like Reinhard telling himself—he wouldn't.
Thus, all Otto could do was clearly state his own stance.
“—Phew, what an outrageous interlude. Never thought in my life I'd have casual chit-chat with the 『Sword Saint』.”
Otto shrugged, showing Reinhard he wouldn't pry into personal matters.
A survival skill honed as a merchant—meeting people of all positions in various lands. No matter what you hear, overtly showing you won't use it declares your harmlessness.
Usually, that let you leave the inn without incident.
“Sometimes at drinking parties, people blurt out info they absolutely shouldn't, then try to silence the listener unreasonably…”
So even in taverns—best places for info—you needed tricks to avoid accidentally learning too much. That merchant wisdom ended up useful even in the Royal Selection—life sure is unpredictable.
In any case—
“Thank you for sharing about Father. I'll keep it in mind.”
Reinhard nodded, accepting Otto's declaration. Otto shrugged again and continued “Sorry about that.”
“Did I just add unnecessary worries? With a position like 『Sword Saint』, you must have plenty already.”
“It's a Divine Protection and position I wished for. That makes it my responsibility.”
Divine Protections were bestowed.
Wished for was an odd way to put it, but Otto immediately realized—only the 『Sword Saint's Divine Protection』 was inherited post-natally by one deemed worthy.
Reinhard too had inherited it after the previous 『Sword Saint's』 death.
“For a Divine Protection holder, worries about living with their blessing never end. From what I see, Otto, you handle yours quite well—maybe not so much?”
“Handle it? Far from it. Born with an uncontrollable Divine Protection that's constantly run me ragged. If I could give it away, I'd happily do so.”
“Haha, careful what you say. Plenty envy Divine Protection holders. Of course, I needn't tell you that.”
“Honestly, I don't think it's anything enviable.”
As Reinhard said, worries about one's Divine Protection never cease—for holders and non-holders alike, even among holders of different blessings. It's a model-less difficult problem you keep answering.
His reply to Reinhard's compliment was Otto's true feeling. —He'd never once gotten conceited thinking he had mastered the Divine Protection of Soul Language. Probably never would.
“Unlike your 『Sword Saint』 Divine Protection, mine isn't the kind that goes down in history.”
“Please don't tense up every time you hear 『Sword Saint』 『Sword Saint』. I'm still immature, swayed by the title. I have no intention of acting superior because of it.”
“They say position shapes the person. Maybe you're unconsciously intimidating others with that very 『Sword Saint』-ness without realizing.”
“Intimidating with 『Sword Saint』-ness… May I hear more details? Perhaps that's why Felt-sama and the others say things to me.”
“Getting pressed hard on casual banter is troubling, you know!”
Unexpectedly hooked, Otto cried out under Reinhard's barrage of questions.
As the exchange lightened and thinned out, Otto slightly lowered his wariness toward Reinhard, making mental room and leeway.
At least for now, Otto had no reason to rush into political maneuvering against Felt or Reinhard at the cost of goodwill. His own condition wasn't great either.
Above all—right now, far away, Subaru was in the midst of agonizing over Al's heart for his sake.
“Even I would feel guilty scheming in the shadows while Natsuki-san and the others are trying to save someone's heart. —Though if it were Marquis Mathers, that'd be different.”
Unlike Subaru and company—who headed to the Pleiades Watchtower with Al—Roswaal had taken Priscilla's attendant Schult and accompanied him to her territory, Bariel. No doubt behind that concern and care lay intent to interfere in Bariel territory at this opportunity.
As a Royal Selection participant, that action was correct, zealous—and contemptible. —At the same time, Otto felt both that he couldn't become that ruthless and that maybe that made him better—or softer.
“Well then, shall we brew the tea? Allow me to show my skill.”
“No holding back—I'll observe your technique.”
Upon reaching the kitchen, Reinhard snatched the tea set from the tray and began preparations. Watching, Otto took a breath in momentary rest amid future developments.
But he had a premonition. A sense that soon—something would move again.
“…Even postponing Emilia-sama and Felt-sama's audience at the castle—some discussion is being prioritized there. Just what is going on?”
As Otto muttered, a small shadow stirred in the shadow of a pillar. —The Zoddah bug they'd made a deal with: report any movement in the castle.
—Otto's premonition proved correct in two ways.
One was the Zoddah bug's report that the castle discussion—deliberately excluding Royal Selection candidates—had reached some conclusion.
The other was a report from Grasis, the girl of Felt's camp who had visited the royal residence.
Trouble had arisen with Subaru and company heading to the Pleiades Watchtower; Al had been restrained, and now Subaru and the others were heading to the royal capital—a complicated, tumultuous report.
